<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaac</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yiṣḥāq, Isḥāq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaac is the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> patriarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and a figure of the Abrahamic religions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> he is the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and Sarah, father of Jacob and Esau, and grandfather of the twelve tribes of Israel. His name means 'he will laugh', recalling the laughter of his parents when his birth was foretold. Genesis tells of his binding on Mount Moriah, his marriage to Rebekah, and his death at 180. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> regards him as a prophet and names him seventeen times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, while some recent scholarship suggests he may originally have been an ancestor revered at a sanctuary in the Beersheba region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac.jpg" alt="Isaac" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerbrand van den Eeckhout · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isaac_Blessing_Jacob_MET_DP145918_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
